To determine how long it takes the marshall to catch up to the robber, we begin by analyzing the situation step by step.

---

### **Step 1: Understand the Head Start**

The robber starts running at a speed of **13 mi/h** and continues for **30 minutes (or 0.5 hours)** before the marshall begins chasing. During this time, the robber covers:

$$
\text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 13 \, \text{mi/h} \times 0.5 \, \text{h} = 6.5 \, \text{miles}
$$

So, when the marshall starts, the robber is **6.5 miles ahead**.

---

### **Step 2: Determine Relative Speed**

After the marshall begins chasing, both are moving in the same direction. The **relative speed** at which the marshall closes the gap is:

$$
\text{Relative Speed} = 16 \, \text{mi/h} - 13 \, \text{mi/h} = 3 \, \text{mi/h}
$$

This means the marshall gains **3 miles per hour** on the robber.

---

### **Step 3: Calculate Time to Catch Up**

We now compute the time it takes for the marshall to close the **6.5-mile gap** at a rate of **3 mi/h**:

$$
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Relative Speed}} = \frac{6.5}{3} = \frac{13}{6} \, \text{hours}
$$

This is the exact value in fractional form.

---

### **Final Answer**

$$
\boxed{\frac{13}{6}}
$$"
