$$

This means the vector $ \beta $ is a linear combination of $ \alpha_2, \alpha_3, \alpha_4 $, and not directly involving $ \alpha_1 $.

The system $ A \mathbf{x} = \beta $ implies:

$$
x_1 \alpha_1 + x_2 \alpha_2 + x_3 \alpha_3 + x_4 \alpha_4 = \beta
$$

Substituting $ \alpha_1 = \alpha_2 - \alpha_3 + \alpha_4 $, we get:

$$
x_1 (\alpha_2 - \alpha_3 + \alpha_4) + x_2 \alpha_2 + x_3 \alpha_3 + x_4 \alpha_4 = \beta
$$

Grouping terms:

$$
(x_1 + x_2)\alpha_2 + (-x_1 + x_3)\alpha_3 + (x_1 + x_4)\alpha_4 = \beta
$$

But from earlier, $ \beta = 3\alpha_2 + 2\alpha_3 + 5\alpha_4 $, so equating coefficients:

$$
x_1 + x_2 = 3 \\
