### **(b) Right-tailed $ z $ value**

We are looking for the z-score such that:

$$
P(Z > z) = \alpha = 0.08 \quad \Rightarrow \quad P(Z < z) = 1 - 0.08 = 0.92
$$

This corresponds to the **92nd percentile** of the standard normal distribution. Again, using standard tables or inverse CDF:

$$
z = 1.40
$$

This is the positive counterpart of the lower-tailed value from part (a), and it's also rounded to two decimal places.

---

### **(c) Two-tailed $ |z| $ value**

For a two-tailed test with significance level $ \alpha = 0.08 $, we split the significance level equally between both tails:

$$
\text{Each tail} = \frac{0.08}{2} = 0.04
$$

So we want the z-score such that:

$$
P(Z > z) = 0.04 \quad \Rightarrow \quad P(Z < z) = 1 - 0.04 = 0.96
$$

This corresponds to the **96th percentile** of the standard normal distribution. From standard tables or inverse CDF:

$$
|z| = 1.75
$$

This value is rounded to two decimal places, consistent with typical statistical reporting.

