\ln(a + b) + 4 \ln(a - b) - 5 \ln c
$$  
as a single logarithm of the form $ \ln A $, we apply the fundamental properties of logarithms:

---

### Step 1: Use Logarithmic Identities

Recall the following identities:
- $ n \ln x = \ln(x^n) $
- $ \ln x + \ln y = \ln(xy) $
- $ \ln x - \ln y = \ln\left(\frac{x}{y}\right) $

Apply these to each term:

- $ 4 \ln(a - b) = \ln[(a - b)^4] $
- $ -5 \ln c = \ln(c^{-5}) = \ln\left(\frac{1}{c^5}\right) $

So the original expression becomes:
$$
\ln(a + b) + \ln[(a - b)^4] + \ln\left(c^{-5}\right)
$$

---

### Step 2: Combine into a Single Logarithm

Using the identity $ \ln x + \ln y + \ln z = \ln(xyz) $, we combine all terms:

$$
\ln\left[(a + b) \cdot (a - b)^4 \cdot c^{-5}\right]
$$

