Using partial fractions:

$$
\frac{1}{n^2(n - 1)} = \frac{1}{n - 1} - \frac{1}{n} - \frac{1}{n^2}
$$

Then:

$$
S = \sum_{n=2}^\infty \left( \frac{1}{n - 1} - \frac{1}{n} - \frac{1}{n^2} \right)
$$

This telescopes:

- $ \sum_{n=2}^\infty \left( \frac{1}{n - 1} - \frac{1}{n} \right) = 1 $
- $ \sum_{n=2}^\infty \frac{1}{n^2} = \zeta(2) - 1 $

So:

$$
S = 1 - (\zeta(2) - 1) = 2 - \zeta(2)
$$

Therefore:

$$
I = 1 - S = 1 - (2 - \zeta(2)) = \zeta(2) - 1
$$

Which matches exactly the value of the integral.

